      "filename": "Winston Churchill Memorial and Library, National Churchill Museum, Fulton, Missouri. USA. Photo by. Yasir Qazi.jpg",
      "title": "File:Winston Churchill Memorial and Library, National Churchill Museum, Fulton, Missouri. USA. Photo by. Yasir Qazi.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3b/Winston_Churchill_Memorial_and_Library%2C_National_Churchill_Museum%2C_Fulton%2C_Missouri._USA._Photo_by._Yasir_Qazi.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Winston_Churchill_Memorial_and_Library,_National_Churchill_Museum,_Fulton,_Missouri._USA._Photo_by._Yasir_Qazi.jpg",
      "width": 4000,
      "height": 3000,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "YasirQazi",
      "description": "Winston Churchill Memorial and Library, National Churchill Museum, Fulton, Missouri. USA. Photo by. Yasir Qazi\nThe National Churchill Museum (formerly the Winston Churchill Memorial and Library), located on the Westminster College campus in Fulton, Missouri, United States, commemorates the life and times of Sir Winston Churchill. In 1946, Winston Churchill delivered his famous \"Sinews of Peace\" address in the Westminster Historic Gymnasium. In it was the line: \"From Stettin in the Baltic to Trieste in the Adriatic an iron curtain has descended across the Continent.\" This sentence caused the oration to become known as the \"Iron Curtain\" speech. \"Sinews of Peace\" heralded the beginning of the Cold War.\nThe National Churchill Museum comprises three distinct but related elements: the Church of St Mary Aldermanbury, the museum, and the \"Breakthrough\" sculpture.\nThe central element of the National Churchill Museum is the Church of St. Mary, Aldermanbury, a 16th-century church moved stone-by-stone to Fulton from the City of London. Today, the church looks much as it did in 1677—carefully restored to recreate the building that architect Christopher Wren designed after the Great Fire of London destroyed the original 12th-century church.\nBeneath the church is the Churchill museum, renovated in 2006. Through interactive new exhibits, the museum tells Churchill's story, discussing his personal and political life and his legacy. Additionally, the Clementine-Spencer Churchill Reading Room houses an extensive research collection about Churchill and his era.\n\nOutside the church stands the \"Breakthrough\" sculpture, formed from eight sections of the Berlin Wall. Churchill's granddaughter, artist Edwina Sandys, designed the sculpture in order to commemorate both the \"Sinews of Peace\" speech and the fall of the Berlin Wall.",
